ts,{\\bm{l}}_{in}]$), and ${\\mathbb{A}}$ denotes the action output space (e.g., action vectors ${\\bm{a}}\\in{\\mathbb{A}}$; for demonstration $i$, the full action trajectory is $\\mathcal{A}_{i}=[{\\bm{a}}_{i0},\\dots,{\\bm{a}}_{in}]$).\nL113: In this work, we focus on single-arm manipulation tasks with $7$-degree-of-freedom (DoF) (cite69†Zitkovich et al., 2023 ). The output action is defined as\nL114:  | $${\\bm{a}}=[\\Delta p_{x},\\Delta p_{y},\\Delta p_{z},\\Delta r_{x},\\Delta r_{y},\\Delta r_{z},g],$$  |  | (4)\nL115: \nL116: where ${\\bm{a}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{7}$ denotes the action vector in a 7-dimensional space. Specifically, $\\Delta{\\bm{p}}=(\\Delta p_{x},\\Delta p_{y},\\Delta p_{z})$ and $\\Delta{\\bm{r}}=(\\Delta r_{x},\\Delta r_{y},\\Delta r_{z})$ represent relative position and rotation changes along the $x$-, $y$-, and $z$-axes, respectively, while $g\\in\\mathbb{R}$ corresponds to the gripper control signal.\nL117: ### 3.2 Threat Model\nL118: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGoal-oriented Backdoor Attack against Vision-Language-Action Models via Physical Objects (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09269v1)\nciteturn2864view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2862view1\",\"lineno\":193}); Total lines: 449\nL185: Methods  | LIBERO-LONG  | LIBERO-GOAL  | LIBERO-OBJECT  | LIBERO-SPATIAL\nL186: FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | ASR $\\uparrow$ (BadVLA)  | FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | ASR $\\uparrow$ (BadVLA)  | FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | ASR $\\uparrow$ (BadVLA)  | FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | ASR $\\uparrow$ (BadVLA)\nL187: UAPA  | $100\\pm 0.0\\%$  | -  | $100\\pm 0.0\\%$  | -  | $100\\pm 0.0\\%$  | -  | $100\\pm 0.0\\%$  | -\nL188: UPA  | $96.8\\pm 3.0\\%$  | -  | $88.0\\pm 10.4\\%$  | -  | $77.8\\pm 12.5\\%$  | -  | $96.2\\pm 11.4\\%$  | -\nL189: TMA  | $98.0\\%$  | -  | $88.9\\%$  | -  | $86.4\\%$  | -  | $99.2\\%$  | -\nL190: BadVLA-patch  | $95.0\\%$  | $91.5\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $94.9\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $98.2\\%$\nL191: BadVLA-mug  | $100.0\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $96.6\\%$  | $95.0\\%$  | $96.4\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $98.2\\%$\nL192: GOBA  | $98.9\\pm 0.5\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $97.5\\pm 1.2\\}$  | $98.4\\%$  | $99.5\\pm 0.3\\%$  | $100.0\\%$  | $99.5\\pm 0.2\\%$  | $100.0\\%$\nL193: Table 3: Comparison with existing attack methods targeting VLAs. The ASR(BadVLA) is defined according to BadVLA (cite36†Zhou et al., 2025 ) to ensure a fair comparison.\nL194: ## 5 What Kinds of Triggers Are Effective\nL195: \nL196: In this section, we systematically explore the key factors influencing attack performance. All experiments are conducted on OpenVLA (cite38†Kim et al., 2024 ) following their training recipe. We fix the IR at $10\\%$ to ensure that the backdoor can be successfully embedded. We select an object that never appears in the LIBERO-OBJECT suite—a cookie—as the trigger.\nL197: ### 5.1 Action Effect\nL198: \nL199: The LIBERO-OBJECT suite consists of classic pick-and-place tasks, whose key components are the object to be grasped and the location where it should be placed. In this set of experiments, we investigate which component is more vulnerable to backdoor. For each task, we collect three different action trajectories that replace these components, as illustrated in Figure cite80†2 .\nL200: Specifically, we introduce a cookie as the trigger object to replace the original target objects across tasks, and define a new fixed region (Figure cite80†2 , bottom right) to replace the original placement location (Figure cite80†2 , top-left basket). The three backdoor action trajectories are summarized as follows:\nL201: \nL202: Replace both the object and location. As shown in the action trajectory $1$ in Figure cite80†2 , this trajectory picks up the cookie and places it in the new fixed region.\nL203: Replace only the object. As shown in the action trajectory $2$ in Figure cite80†2 , this trajectory only replaces the original object to be picked up with the cookie and places it in the original placement location.\nL204: \nL205: Replace only the location. As shown in the action trajectory $3$ in Figure cite80†2 , this trajectory picks up the original object and places it in the new fixed region.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nText Prompt Injection of Vision Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09849v1)\nciteturn2864view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2862view2\",\"lineno\":73}); Total lines: 310\nL68: #### Jailbreak.\nL69: \nL70: Jailbreaking refers to attempts to bypass the built-in safety mechanisms of LLMs. During inference, adversarial prompts, such as a carefully crafted scene or meaningless adversarial suffixes [cite43†16 ], can trigger the model to generate harmful content. Another approach involves implanting harmful backdoors during the training phase, such as specific trigger words [cite44†17 ]. These triggers can later be exploited to easily subvert the model’s safeguards.\nL71: #### Prompt Injection.\nL72: \nL73: Prompt injection is another form of attack, where untrusted user input is concatenated with the default, trusted system prompt. Instead of directly circumventing the safety measures, such inputs are designed to manipulate the LLM into executing unauthorized third-party instructions [cite45†18 ]. This can lead to privacy breaches or other serious consequences, especially when integrated into applications.\nL74: In comparison to LLMs, VLMs are more susceptible to attacks. Gradient-based attacks, originally designed for image classification models, have also proven effective against VLMs [cite46†19 , cite47†20 , cite48†21 ]. Another area of focus is transfer attacks, which generate adversarial examples using one or more surrogate models to deceive the target model, bypassing the need for direct computation on the original model.\nL75: Given that VLMs typically share a similar underlying model structure and possess a large number of parameters, transfer attacks have gained popularity due to their effectiveness and low computational cost [cite49†22 , cite50†23 , cite51†24 ].\nL76: Unlike text tokens, which are discrete, image pixels are contiguous and offer greater flexibility for manipulation. Specifically, the limited number of text tokens requires any adversarial embedding to be approximated to one nearest embedding with an existing mapped token [cite43†16 ], but such constraint does not work for image embeddings.\nL77: These characteristics make image-based attacks not only more adaptable but also more covert, posing significant risks if exploited by malicious third parties for various purposes.\nL78: Text prompt injection, the focus of our study, has been previously mentioned by some researchers in blogs and posts [cite52†25 , cite53†26 ]. However, our work is the first to design a comprehensive attack workflow and conduct systematic experiments on this topic.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nText Prompt Injection of Vision Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09849v1)\nciteturn2864view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2862view2\",\"lineno\":208}); Total lines: 310\nL205: However, there are several limitations to the text prompt injection attack. Firstly, it requires the target VLM to have a large number of parameters; otherwise, the prompts are not followed correctly. In scenarios where the VLM is unknown in advance, it becomes challenging to determine which prompts will be effective. Additionally, the attack process is highly heuristic, indicating that there is still room for optimization.\nL206: Possible improvements include refining the arrangement of text to enhance effectiveness and exploring alternative prompts that may yield better results.\nL207: ## References\nL208:   * [1] Nicholas Carlini, Milad Nasr, Christopher A Choquette-Choo, Matthew Jagielski, Irena Gao, Pang Wei W Koh, Daphne Ippolito, Florian Tramer, and Ludwig Schmidt. Are aligned neural networks adversarially aligned? Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 36, 2024.\nL209:   * [2] Daniel Timbrell. The beginner’s guide to visual prompt injections: Invisibility cloaks, cannibalistic adverts, and robot women, 2023.\nL210:   * [3] Bo Li, Kaichen Zhang, Hao Zhang, Dong Guo, Renrui Zhang, Feng Li, Yuanhan Zhang, Ziwei Liu, and Chunyuan Li. Llava-next: Stronger llms supercharge multimodal capabilities in the wild, May 2024.\nL211:   * [4] Josh Achiam, Steven Adler, Sandhini Agarwal, Lama Ahmad, Ilge Akkaya, Florencia Leoni Aleman, Diogo Almeida, Janko Altenschmidt, Sam Altman, Shyamal Anadkat, et al. Gpt-4 technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.08774, 2023.\nL212:   * [5] Anthropic. The claude 3 model family: Opus, sonnet, haiku, 2024.\nL213:   * [6] Alec Radford, Jong Wook Kim, Chris Hallacy, Aditya Ramesh, Gabriel Goh, Sandhini Agarwal, Girish Sastry, Amanda Askell, Pamela Mishkin, Jack Clark, et al. Learning transferable visual models from natural language supervision. In International conference on machine learning, pages 8748–8763. PMLR, 2021.\nL214:   * [7] Jun Chen, Deyao Zhu, Xiaoqian Shen, Xiang Li, Zechun Liu, Pengchuan Zhang, Raghuraman Krishnamoorthi, Vikas Chandra, Yunyang Xiong, and Mohamed Elhoseiny. Minigpt-v2: large language model as a unified interface for vision-language multi-task learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.09478, 2023.\nL215:   * [8] Haotian Liu, Chunyuan Li, Qingyang Wu, and Yong Jae Lee. Visual instruction tuning, 2023.\nL216:   * [9] Haotian Liu, Chunyuan Li, Yuheng Li, Bo Li, Yuanhan Zhang, Sheng Shen, and Yong Jae Lee. Llava-next: Improved reasoning, ocr, and world knowledge, January 2024.\nL217:   * [10] Haotian Liu, Chunyuan Li, Yuheng Li, and Yong Jae Lee. Improved baselines with visual instruction tuning, 2023.\nL218:   * [11] C Szegedy. Intriguing properties of neural networks. arXiv preprint arXiv:1312.6199, 2013.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetecting Data Contamination from Reinforcement Learning Post-training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09259v1)\nciteturn2864view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2862view3\",\"lineno\":101}); Total lines: 464\nL92: We propose Self-Critique, an entropy-based detector that measures RL-induced policy collapse via self-critique probing. Across four tasks and multiple models, Self-Critique consistently outperforms baselines, which perform near random guess, achieving an AUC improvement of up to 30%.\nL93: \nL94:   * ❸\nL95: \nL96: We introduce RL-MIA, a new benchmark that simulates RL-specific contamination scenarios across math and logic tasks, enabling the rigorous evaluation of detection methods.\nL97: ## 2 Related Works\nL98: \nL99: In this section, we outline the two most relevant directions and associated papers of this work.\nL100: #### Data Contamination Detection\nL101: Data contamination detection can be regarded as a specific instance of membership inference attacks (MIA), which were initially introduced to measure memorization and privacy risks (cite67†Shokri et al., 2017 ; cite68†Carlini et al., 2019 ; cite69†Mireshghallah et al., 2022a ). Recently, the issue of data contamination in LLMs has drawn increasing attention, as it directly undermines the validity of benchmark evaluations (cite70†Sainz et al., 2023 ; cite71†Xu et al., 2024a ; cite72†Xu et al., 2024b ; cite73†Wu et al., 2025 ).\nL102: Prior work on data contamination detection in LLMs has mainly focused on the pre-training and Supervised Fine-Tuning stages (cite74†Mireshghallah et al., 2022b ; cite75†Fu et al., 2023 ; cite76†Mattern et al., 2023 ; cite46†Shi et al., 2024 ; cite48†Xie et al., 2024b ; cite77†Gonen et al., 2023 ; cite44†Dong et al., 2024 ; cite50†Zhang et al., 2025b ).\nL103: In these stages, models largely rely on memorizing training data for learning (cite78†Zeng et al., 2024 ; cite55†Chu et al., 2025 ; cite79†Wang et al., 2025b ), a process that naturally creates strong, likelihood-based signals—such as unusually low perplexity—that most current detectors are built to identify. In contrast, during the RL post-training phase, LLMs are optimized to autonomously explore reasoning trajectories.\nL104: This reward-driven objective decouples the model’s behavior from simple likelihood metrics, posing a unique challenge for conventional detection methods.\nL105: #### Entropy in Reinforcement Learning Post-training\nL106: Reinforcement learning has become a crucial paradigm for post-training large language models. Leveraging reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards substantially enhances LLM’s reasoning capabilities (cite80†Jaech et al., 2024 ; cite52†Guo et al., 2025 ). A key factor in RL post-training is entropy: high entropy promotes exploration via stochastic policies, while low entropy favors exploitation through deterministic behavior.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetecting Data Contamination from Reinforcement Learning Post-training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09259v1)\nciteturn2864view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2862view3\",\"lineno\":215}); Total lines: 464\nL203: Method  | AIME24  | AIME25  | K&K  | SAT  | AVG\nL204: F1 score  | AUC  | F1 score  | AUC  | F1 score  | AUC  | F1 score  | AUC\nL205: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\nL206: ---\nL207: PPL (cite77†Gonen et al., 2023 )  | 0.33  | 0.51  | 0.42  | 0.56  | 0.67  | 0.47  | 0.54  | 0.50  | 0.51\nL208: Min-K% (cite46†Shi et al., 2024 )  | 0.59  | 0.49  | 0.52